# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
aristotle-function.md:1: argue/readings: S1 is read 2 ways: aristotle-function.logic.r3 and aristotle-function.logic.r3.2 have "carpenter_work" and "cobbler_work" and "members_work"; aristotle-function.logic.r3.3 has "carpenter_work" and "cobbler_work" and "members_work" and "members_work → man_work"; they differ when only "the carpenter has certain works and courses of action", "the cobbler has certain works and courses of action" and "eye, hand, foot and generally each of man's members manifestly has some special work" are true, which the first allows
aristotle-function.md:1: argue/answered: S1: answered no, which keeps aristotle-function.logic.r3.3, to: Suppose "the carpenter has certain works and courses of action", "the cobbler has certain works and courses of action" and "eye, hand, foot and generally each of man's members manifestly has some special work" are true and "Man as Man, the whole Man as distinct from his members, has some work of his own and is not left by Nature without a work" is false. Is S1, "Are we then to suppose, that while carpenter and cobbler have certain works and courses of action, Man as Man has none, but is left by Nature without a work? or would not one rather hold, that as eye, hand, and foot, and generally each of his members, has manifestly some special work; so too the whole Man, as distinct from all these, has some work of his own?", true then?
aristotle-function.logic.r3: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: aristotle-function.logic.r3 has "carpenter_work ∧ cobbler_work ∧ members_work → man_work"; aristotle-function.logic.r3.2 has nothing from it; they differ when only "the carpenter has certain works and courses of action", "the cobbler has certain works and courses of action" and "eye, hand, foot and generally each of man's members manifestly has some special work" are true, which the second allows; aristotle-function.logic.r3.3 has "carpenter_work ∧ cobbler_work → man_work"
aristotle-function.logic.r3: argue/readings: U1: aristotle-function.logic.r3 and aristotle-function.logic.r3.3 take for granted "if (the carpenter has certain works and courses of action and the cobbler has certain works and courses of action and eye, hand, foot and generally each of man's members manifestly has some special work), then Man as Man, the whole Man as distinct from his members, has some work of his own and is not left by Nature without a work"; aristotle-function.logic.r3.2 does not
aristotle-function.logic.r3: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps aristotle-function.logic.r3 and aristotle-function.logic.r3.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if (the carpenter has certain works and courses of action and the cobbler has certain works and courses of action and eye, hand, foot and generally each of man's members manifestly has some special work), then Man as Man, the whole Man as distinct from his members, has some work of his own and is not left by Nature without a work"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
aristotle-function.logic.r3: argue/readings: U2: aristotle-function.logic.r3 and aristotle-function.logic.r3.3 take for granted "if (the carpenter has certain works and courses of action and the cobbler has certain works and courses of action), then Man as Man, the whole Man as distinct from his members, has some work of his own and is not left by Nature without a work"; aristotle-function.logic.r3.2 does not
aristotle-function.logic.r3: argue/answered: U2: answered yes, which keeps aristotle-function.logic.r3 and aristotle-function.logic.r3.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if (the carpenter has certain works and courses of action and the cobbler has certain works and courses of action), then Man as Man, the whole Man as distinct from his members, has some work of his own and is not left by Nature without a work"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
